ef 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419681 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419681 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "32c807d8-09ff-597f-a633-55d26150ec43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419682 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "80639a3b-2c96-57ee-bd01-981e0851478b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419684 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419684 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419684 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "662e0d41-a6b8-58b3-ad4a-e835c48f17e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419684 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419685 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419685 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419685 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419685 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7afd5a54-c223-5e70-9582-9406545b2d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419685 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419687 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419687 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b2e1e0a-a93e-5b2b-ba4a-b6011da00e9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419687 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419688 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b0f643e-dd6c-5eaf-875c-1b54d4c6e171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49084fac-fe76-5f2f-8b13-058b89e81921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a18e940-4aa2-5db0-8cbe-98d6ec06c6cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf368b08-5b21-5ea2-9201-b8d34ec52b51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186448614 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448614 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448614 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03a7c2b7-b4a3-5168-84dc-aab27f106193" #edb/ref 17592186